Subject: [PATCH 0012/0012] pin one handoff frame, the window the 55 fps series measured


The compositor imports the planes only when the queued add/create_immed
message reaches it, and by then the decoder has retired the frame and handed
its capture buffer to the next decode, which makes the import fail with
"Failed to import plane 0 as R8". The export's own duplicate keeps the
dma-buf object alive but not the decoder's surface, so the handoff needs one
pinned frame across the message.

A ring of zero was a theory that the duplicate is enough; it is not. A ring of
24 was measured to break the stream: it pins the whole capture pool, so the
decoder names a fresh DMA-BUF for every frame, the presenter's cache misses
every frame, the compositor EGL-imports a new image every frame and the driver
eventually refuses one of them. One is what the r0.44 series ran at 55 fps and
is what the knob now defaults to; OPENNOW_HANDOFF_RETAINED still overrides it
over 0..=24 so both ends of the measurement can be repeated.
---
 .../src/frame_producer.rs                     | 56 ++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)

diff --git a/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform-linux/src/frame_producer.rs b/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform-linux/src/frame_producer.rs
index f8dc01e6..211025db 100644
--- a/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform-linux/src/frame_producer.rs
+++ b/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform-linux/src/frame_producer.rs
@@ -27,25 +27,29 @@ const DECODE_WAIT_TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(1);
 /// and the dma-buf fds the decoder opened for them. The export owns its own
 /// `F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC` duplicate of the decoder's handle and the presenter keeps
 /// that duplicate open until the flush which carries the descriptor succeeds,
-/// so the compositor has its own reference to the dma-buf and the import does
-/// not need this crate to hold the decoder's frame at all.
+/// so the compositor has its own reference to the dma-buf. That reference
+/// keeps the object alive, but not the decoder's surface: the compositor
+/// imports the planes only when the queued `add`/`create_immed` message
+/// reaches it, and by then the decoder has already retired the frame and
+/// handed the capture buffer to the next decode. The import then fails with
+/// "Failed to import plane 0 as R8" and the protocol error closes the stream
+/// window.
 ///
-/// Holding it anyway costs the decoder a capture buffer for as long as the
-/// ring keeps it, and this board's decoder has very few. `hantro-vpu` allocates
-/// the capture buffers from contiguous memory and fails the next
-/// `VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS` with `Cannot allocate memory (12)` once a handful are
-/// outstanding: the stream then stops at the frames already presented while
-/// FFmpeg retries `Failed setup for format drm_prime` forever, and the frames
-/// that were in flight when the decoder re-initialised name closed descriptors
-/// (`fstat on DMA-BUF failed: Bad file descriptor (os error 9)`). The default
-/// is therefore no retention: the duplicate in [`export_dmabuf`] is what keeps
-/// the buffer alive for the compositor, exactly as the r0.41 series did when it
-/// presented this board at 55 fps.
+/// One pinned frame is enough to keep the surface, and the fds it named, alive
+/// across that handoff, and it is what the r0.44 series measured: pinning a
+/// wide window instead pins the whole capture pool, so the decoder names a
+/// fresh DMA-BUF for every frame, the presenter's one-compositor-buffer-per-
+/// DMA-BUF cache misses every frame, the compositor EGL-imports a new image
+/// every frame, and the driver eventually refuses one of those imports (the
+/// same fatal "importing the supplied dmabufs failed"). `hantro-vpu` also
+/// allocates its capture buffers from contiguous memory and fails
+/// `VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS` with `Cannot allocate memory (12)` once too many are
+/// outstanding. A ring of 24 was measured to break the stream that way.
 ///
-/// `OPENNOW_HANDOFF_RETAINED=N` still asks for a ring of N frames for the A/B
-/// runs, clamped to 0..=24, so the measurement that established this default
-/// can be repeated without a rebuild.
-const COMPOSITOR_HANDOFF_RETAINED: usize = 0;
+/// `OPENNOW_HANDOFF_RETAINED=N` asks for another window for the A/B runs,
+/// clamped to 0..=24 so both the "no retention" and the "too much retention"
+/// ends of the measurement can be repeated without a rebuild.
+const COMPOSITOR_HANDOFF_RETAINED: usize = 1;
 
 /// The handoff window in force: [`COMPOSITOR_HANDOFF_RETAINED`] unless
 /// `OPENNOW_HANDOFF_RETAINED=N` asks for another one for the measurement.
@@ -940,15 +944,17 @@ impl LinuxFrameProducer {
 
     /// Keeps the decoder frame whose DMA-BUF went to a compositor alive.
     ///
-    /// [`export_dmabuf`] duplicates the descriptor the kernel exported, and
-    /// the duplicate is what keeps the buffer alive for the compositor; the
-    /// frame itself is not needed once the call returns. A non-empty ring
-    /// additionally pins the newest N frames, which costs the decoder one
-    /// capture buffer per entry; see [`COMPOSITOR_HANDOFF_RETAINED`] for why
-    /// the default ring is empty on this board.
+    /// [`export_dmabuf`] duplicates the descriptor the kernel exported, which
+    /// keeps the dma-buf object alive for the compositor, but the compositor
+    /// imports the planes only when the queued request reaches it. By then the
+    /// decoder has retired this frame and handed its capture buffer to the
+    /// next decode, and the import fails. The ring pins the newest N frames so
+    /// the surface, and the fds it named, survive the handoff; it costs the
+    /// decoder one capture buffer per entry, which is why N is small. See
+    /// [`COMPOSITOR_HANDOFF_RETAINED`].
     fn retain_handoff_frame(&mut self, frame: &DecodedVideoFrame) {
-        // No ring means the export's own descriptor duplicate is the only
-        // reference the handoff needs; see `COMPOSITOR_HANDOFF_RETAINED`.
+        // A zero-width ring is the A/B case that measures the failure mode:
+        // the export's duplicate is then the only reference the handoff has.
         if self.handoff_frames.is_empty() {
             return;
         }
